---
metaTitle: Provident Numismatic Authentication | Coin grading and authentication
metaDescription: >-
  Independent authentication, attribution and grading of US, world and ancient
  coins, tokens and medals. Venice, Florida.
eyebrow: Venice, Florida
heading: Authentication, attribution and grading for coins of every age.
intro: >-
  We examine United States, world and ancient coins, tokens and medals, record
  what we find, and seal each piece in a holder made to protect it, under a
  label made to suit it.
primaryButton: Our process
secondaryButton: Verify a certificate
gradeTitle: Four fields. One standard of care.
gradeIntro: >-
  From the colonial era to the present day, from Rome to the modern mints of the
  world, every piece we accept passes through the same sequence of examination,
  record and encapsulation.
grades:
  - mark: coin
    title: United States
    text: >-
      United States coinage, including colonial issues, errors and fractional
      gold.
  - mark: globe
    title: World
    text: >-
      Coins of the world from 1600 to the present day, attributed to issuer,
      denomination and date.
  - mark: laurel
    title: Ancient
    text: >-
      Roman Republican and Imperial coins and select Greek types, with expanded
      identification, weight and subgrades for strike and surface where the
      service includes them.
  - mark: medal
    title: Tokens and medals
    text: >-
      Most tokens and medals, examined, attributed and graded through the same
      process as coinage.
approachTitle: The coin first. Everything else in its service.
approachText: |-
  A grade is only as useful as the care behind it. Every coin we receive is logged, examined for authenticity, attributed and graded before it is sealed, and each result is kept on record under its own certificate number.

  The holder and the label matter too. A coin that has survived centuries deserves to be presented with the same seriousness with which it was examined.
principles:
  - mark: balance
    title: Rigour
    text: The same examination for every coin, whatever its value.
  - mark: seal
    title: Preservation
    text: >-
      Sealed in a tamper-evident holder, so the coin passes to the next
      collector unchanged.
  - mark: pen
    title: Considered design
    text: >-
      Holders and labels designed around the coin, with themes drawn from its
      history.
  - mark: registry
    title: Open records
    text: Every certificate on record, and open to anyone who holds the coin.
film:
  - title: Examined under raking light.
    text: >-
      Light drawn low across the surface finds what a direct light hides: wear on
      the high points, hairlines, the marks of a tool.
  - title: Then recorded, and sealed.
    text: >-
      Only once a coin has been authenticated, attributed and graded is it sealed
      and given its certificate number.
processTitle: The process, at a glance.
processText: >-
  Nine steps, from the moment a package is opened to the moment it leaves our
  hands.
holderTitle: A holder that completes the coin.
holderText: |-
  Each coin is sealed in a clear holder welded around it, forming a permanent, tamper-evident seal. Both sides stay in full view.

  The label carries the attribution, the grade and the certificate number, set in a design chosen to suit the coin: themed designs for United States, world, Roman and Greek coins, or one created for a collection.
verifyTitle: Every certificate, on record.
verifyText: >-
  Type the number from any of our labels to see the record we keep for that
  coin, and compare it with the coin in your hand.
closingTitle: Every coin examined. Every result recorded. Every certificate open to inspection.
closingText: Provident Numismatic Authentication, Venice, Florida.
---
